@page "/HowItWorks/options"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/options">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Matching</h2>
            <p>
                Options of the same series (same underlying, type, strike and expiry) follow the same
                <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">matching rules</a> as shares, including same day,
                30 days, the Section 104 pool and short sales (CG55535). Options are reported under "Other property, assets
                and gains".
            </p>
            <p>
                An option can be traded, exercised, assigned and expire on the same day, and only part of a position can be
                assigned. The app combines all of that day's trades in the series and matches each outcome in proportion
                to its quantity.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Writing (selling) an option</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Granting an option is a disposal of the option itself (CG55590).</li>
                <li>
                    The full premium, less the incidental costs of selling it, is taxed as a gain in the tax year the option
                    is written (CG55536). The app does this even if the position is still open.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If you buy the option back, or it is assigned or cash settled, in the same tax year, the closing cost is
                    deducted as normal.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If that happens in a <strong>later</strong> tax year, the closing cost is not deducted from the later
                    year. Instead it is listed in the <strong>tax refund table</strong> on the
                    <a href="CgtYearlyTaxSummaryPage" class="link-styled">CGT Yearly Summary</a> against the year the option
                    was written. It is not a capital loss, so you claim it separately for that year. If you would rather
                    deduct it directly in the year the option was written, for example because you had not yet filed that
                    return, tick "Deduct closing cost of written options in the tax year the option was written" on the
                    <a href="CalculatePage" class="link-styled">Calculate &amp; export</a> page.
                </li>
                <li>If a written option expires, you keep the premium and nothing is deducted.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Buying an option</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Selling it is a normal disposal matched against the purchase.</li>
                <li>
                    If it expires, the full premium paid is an allowable cost against nil proceeds, giving a loss
                    (CG55415, CG55416).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Exercise and assignment</h2>
            <p>
                When an option is settled by delivery of the underlying shares, the grant or purchase and the exercise are
                treated as a single transaction (CG55590 for the writer, CG55600 for the holder). The option itself has no
                tax effect, and its premium moves to the share trade:
            </p>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>What happened</th><th>Effect on the share trade</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>You exercise a call you bought</td><td>Premium and exercise costs are added to the cost of the shares bought</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>You exercise a put you bought</td><td>Premium and exercise costs are deducted from the proceeds of the shares sold</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>A call you wrote is assigned</td><td>Premium received, less assignment costs, is added to the proceeds of the shares sold</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>A put you wrote is assigned</td><td>Premium received, less assignment costs, is deducted from the cost of the shares bought</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                If a written option was taxed in an earlier year and is then assigned, the premium already taxed is listed in
                the tax refund table, because it is now part of the share trade instead.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Cash-settled options</strong> (for example index options) have no share trade. The cash paid or
                received on exercise or assignment is used as the option's own proceeds or cost.
            </p>
            <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                When importing, every exercise or assignment must have a matching share trade or cash settlement on the same
                day in your files. If one is missing, the import stops with an error naming the option, which usually
                means a statement is missing.
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55535" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG55535</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55536" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CG55536</a>: traded options</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55590" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG55590</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55600" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CG55600</a>: the grantor and the grantee</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55415" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG55415</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg55416" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CG55416</a>: losses on options that are abandoned</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
